-**Parameters:**
-- **Threshold ($\tau$):** The opacity value at which the ray terminates. Common values are $1.0$ (full opacity) or $0.95$ (95% opacity, allowing 5% transparency) [S4, unofficial][S14, book]. A lower threshold increases speed but may introduce visible artifacts or bias [S10, unofficial].
-- **Step Size ($\Delta t$):** Distance between samples. Can be fixed or adaptive based on volume density [S16, unofficial].
-
-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-- **Systematic Bias:** Using a fixed threshold $\tau < 1.0$ or even $\tau = 1.0$ with discrete sampling introduces bias because the ray stops before reaching true infinite opacity or the back of the volume, ignoring the background contribution correctly [S6, unofficial][S16, unofficial].
-- **Precision:** Floating-point precision errors in $\alpha_{acc}$ accumulation can cause premature or delayed termination.
-- **Back-to-Front Order:** ERT is invalid for back-to-front compositing because opacity accumulates from the back, and the front samples are not yet known to occlude the rest [S17].
-- **Object-Parallel Rendering:** In object-parallel schemes, ERT is local to each processor and cannot skip voxels that are locally visible but globally occluded by other processors' data [S16, unofficial].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### [Conservative Early Ray Termination](1949-conservative-early-ray-termination.md)
-Uses a lookup table and subscreen aggregation to prune cells during scan conversion without per-pixel state, suitable for parallel rasterization.
+Conservative Early Ray Termination (CERT) is an optimization for the scan conversion phase of volume rendering that prunes the processing of cells by determining if a ray is already sufficiently opaque before the cell is rasterized. It differs from standard Early Ray Termination (ERT) [1019-early-ray-termination.md], which typically requires sequential accumulation from the near plane, by using a lookup table (ERT-table) and subscreen-based aggregation to make visibility decisions based on partially accumulated opacity without per-pixel overhead. This allows the technique to be applied during the parallel scan conversion phase where global ray state is not readily available.
 
 ### [Early Termination for Gaussian Splatting](4764-early-termination-for-gaussian-splatting.md)
-Applies transmittance-based decay to Gaussian splats, allowing sort-free rendering with early exit for negligible Gaussians.
+Early Termination (ET) is a rendering acceleration method for 3D Gaussian Splatting that stops the front-to-back blending process for a pixel once its accumulated opacity or transmittance reaches a predefined threshold, thereby eliminating unnecessary computation for distant or occluded Gaussians. Unlike its parent method, Early Ray Termination, which relies on strict depth sorting to ensure correct alpha-blending order, ET in Gaussian Splatting can be applied to order-independent or sort-free variants by leveraging physically grounded transmittance models that decay similarly to standard alpha-blending. It sets itself apart from siblings like Conservative Early Ray Termination by potentially offering higher throughput gains at the cost of slight visual approximations, as it may terminate rays even if the strict transmittance threshold hasn't been fully met in non-physical blending kernels.
 
 ### [Early Ray Termination with Binary Mask](6641-early-ray-termination-with-binary-mask.md)
-Uses a binary mask to jump to non-empty regions and terminates when accumulated transparency falls below a threshold.
+An acceleration technique for alpha-blending that uses a binary volume to skip fully transparent cells and terminates the front-to-back evaluation when accumulated transparency falls below a threshold. It sets itself apart from standard early ray termination by explicitly encoding the presence of opaque voxels into a bit-mask structure, allowing the renderer to jump directly to non-empty regions rather than sampling every voxel along the ray.
 
 ### [Hybrid Surface-Volume Rendering with Masking and Early Ray Termination](8155-hybrid-surface-volume-rendering-with-masking-and-early-ray-t.md)
-Combines volume ERT with surface masking for hybrid rendering of organs and instruments.
+This method accelerates the hybrid rendering of a volume-rendered organ and a surface-rendered instrument by combining early ray termination for the volume data with spatial masking in the sheared image space for the instrument. It solves the performance bottleneck of real-time surgical simulators where a moving instrument must be composited over a large volume dataset. Unlike standard early ray termination which only stops volume traversal, this method explicitly tracks the instrument's motion in 2D sheared space to limit recomputation to only the affected pixels, achieving speed-ups of up to 2.84x compared to full recomputation.
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-- **Time Complexity:** Reduces the dependence on depth complexity. For opaque-only environments, it eliminates dependence on depth complexity, making cost nearly linear with image size [S9, unofficial]. In general, it reduces execution time by a factor of 5 to 11 compared to brute-force ray casting [S16, unofficial].
-- **Memory Complexity:** Minimal overhead; requires only per-pixel accumulators ($C_{acc}, \alpha_{acc}$) [S16, unofficial].
-- **Performance:** Savings are proportional to the area of the volume intersected by the 3D object and the closeness of the object to the camera [S7]. Highly effective for scenes with opaque or semi-transparent surfaces where voxels away from surfaces have zero opacity [S1].
-- **Strengths:** Significant speedup for dense or opaque volumes; simple to implement; exploits spatial coherence [S15, unofficial].
-- **Limitations:** Introduces systematic bias in the rendered image [S6, unofficial][S16, unofficial]; requires front-to-back traversal order [S17]; less effective in object-parallel rendering where global occlusion is not known [S16, unofficial]; performance is dataset and classification dependent [S13, unofficial].
-- **When to Use:** When rendering volumes with significant opaque or semi-transparent regions and real-time or interactive performance is required.
-- **When Not to Use:** When unbiased physical accuracy is critical (use Russian Roulette instead) or when using back-to-front compositing.
+*   **Time Complexity:** Reduces the dependence on depth complexity. For opaque-only environments, it eliminates dependence on depth complexity entirely, making the cost nearly linear with image size [S9, unofficial]. In general, it reduces the number of samples processed per ray, with savings proportional to the area of the volume intersected by the 3D object and the closeness of the object to the camera [S7][S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** No additional memory structures are required beyond the standard ray state accumulators [S1].
+*   **Measured Performance:** Can reduce execution time by a factor of 5 to 11 for suitable datasets [S18, unofficial]. Savings of up to an order of magnitude over brute-force rendering have been observed for scenes with opaque or semitransparent surfaces [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, highly effective for scenes with opaque objects or dense semi-transparent structures, exploits coherence [S2, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Introduces systematic bias and loss of high-frequency details [S6, unofficial][S10, unofficial]. Requires front-to-back rendering order [S14, unofficial]. Performance is dependent on the dataset and classification used [S15, unofficial]. In object-parallel rendering, ERT is localized per processor (local ERT) rather than global, failing to skip voxels that are locally visible but globally occluded [S18, unofficial].
+*   **When to Use:** When rendering opaque or semi-transparent volumes where distant samples contribute negligibly to the final color.
+*   **When Not to Use:** When unbiased illumination is required (use Russian Roulette instead) [S6, unofficial][S10, unofficial], or when rendering back-to-front.
 
 ## Background
-The idea of adaptively terminating ray tracing was first proposed in [34] (Whitted 1980) [S1][S8, unofficial]. Many medical data algorithms stopped after the first surface or opaque voxel [S1][S14, book]. The specific term "Early Ray Termination" and its application to direct volume rendering with a user-selected threshold was introduced by Krüger and Westermann in 2003 [S7]. It is widely used in GPU-based volume ray casting, especially with Shader Model 3 and later, which allow breaking out of ray-casting loops directly [S3, unofficial]. It is often combined with empty space skipping and space-leaping [S2, unofficial][S12, unofficial].
+The idea of adaptively terminating ray tracing was first proposed in [34] (Whitted 1980) [S1][S8, unofficial]. Many algorithms for displaying medical data stopped after encountering the first surface or the first opaque voxel, reported in [15, 30, 32] [S1]. The specific technique of Early Ray Termination as known today was introduced by Krüger and Westermann in 2003 [S7]. Older GPUs (pre-Shader Model 3) required multi-pass rendering with early-z testing to achieve this, while Shader Model 3 enabled breaking out of the ray-casting loop directly [S3, unofficial]. It is also known as $\alpha$-acceleration [S6, unofficial].
 
